# OPERATIONS — Craftmind League

The league's durable operational reference: how the world, the server, and
the serving stack actually behave, what to do, and what never to do. Every
fact here cites its source entry in `pulse/ledger.jsonl` (by entry `id`)
or a named record file. Build narrative lives in the lane records and the
ledger; this file states only what is current and actionable.

World: Paper 26.1.2 on the Orin (`~/minecraft-server/`), offline mode,
survival. League home: `~/craftmind-league/` on the Thor. World spawn
(32, 70, −96); spawn protection radius 16.

---

## 1. Playerdata and the sample-clone (Paper 26.1.2)

Playerdata for this world lives at `world/players/data/` — a two-level
path under the world folder, one `.dat` per player UUID. (Ledger:
`store-hunt-verdict-20261107`.)

**The sample-clone.** When a player joins with no loadable `.dat` of
their own, the server does not spawn them fresh. Paper's
`PlayerList.placeNewPlayer` calls `loadRandomisedPlayerDataFile`, which
copies a **random existing `.dat` from `world/players/data/`**,
randomises inventory / recipe book / seen-credits, relocates `Pos` by a
cube-root-scaled ordinal offset around the sampled position, and zeroes
`Motion`. On disconnect, the copy is **written back over the joiner's own
filename** — so a cloned state re-files itself under the joiner's name
and the next join inherits it again. The behaviour is self-sealing: it
survives deleting the joiner's file and it survives a full server
restart, because the state is re-derived from the pool on disk at every
join. (Ledger: `store-hunt-verdict-20261107`,
`server-restart-cure-verdict-20261107`.)

Consequences that are now standing knowledge:

- A brand-new name is not a clean control. A fresh bot joining this
  world clones the resident sample (measured: fresh name `CodyProbe2`
  spawned mid-air at the resident sample's position with an offset,
  20/20). Always interpret a "fresh entity" result against this.
  (Ledger: `store-hunt-dual-join-20261107`,
  `store-hunt-verdict-20261107`.)
- **Standing rule: a citizen's own `.dat` must never be absent while
  the sample pool is populated.** With the pool present and the
  citizen's file missing, the next join sample-clones and only luck
  decides the template. The citizen's existing healthy file is the
  protection. (Ledger: `the-cure-persistence-verdict-20261107`.)
- **There is no config off-switch on this build.** A bytecode and
  config-surface hunt over the 26.1.2 jar and this install's configs
  found no playerdata-sample gate. `spigot.yml`'s `sample-count: 12`
  is the server-ping player sample — a different setting; do not touch
  it for this. The standing rule above is the mitigation.
  (Ledger: `the-cure-toggle-finding-20261107`.)
- **Search trap (the three-lane error):** a filesystem search scoped
  `-path "*players/data*"` run against the server root silently
  matches nothing real — it can only match a `world/playerdata` path,
  which does not exist here. Three separate hunts reported the
  playerdata directory empty on the strength of that pattern while
  `world/players/data/` held the pool the whole time. Verify
  playerdata questions by listing `world/players/data/` directly.
  (Ledger: `store-hunt-verdict-20261107`.)
- Pool size: the store hunt counted ~170 `.dat` files; the cure's
  sha256 manifest counted 91 entries at the moment of the pool move.
  These two ledger figures are not reconciled — treat the manifest
  (`pulse/cure-pool-manifest-20261107.txt`) as authoritative for the
  cure, and recount before any future pool operation.
  (Ledger: `store-hunt-verdict-20261107`,
  `the-cure-pool-move-20261107`.)

## 2. Diagnosing a frozen citizen — the corrected signature

**Correction (2026-11-08).** `Motion = [0, −0.0784000015258789, 0]` is
**not** a disease signature. That value is the vanilla first-fall-tick
constant and is written for every fresh spawn: it appears identically
in a proven-healthy fresh file (NBT parse: Pos [29.5, 75, −101.5],
Health 20, food 20, OnGround 1) and in the frozen file. Any league
document that cites the Motion value as the frozen-state signature is
superseded by this section; the affected passages in
`pulse/README.md` carry dated correction pointers to this entry.
(Ledger: `the-cure-persistence-verdict-20261107`,
`the-cure-verdict-20261107`.)

The exhibits that actually diagnose the frozen state in a saved file
are: **`Pos` at an airborne altitude, `OnGround` 0, and drained
vitals** (the frozen file: Pos y = 78.0, Health 12.83, food 13).
(Ledger: `the-cure-verdict-20261107`,
`character-body-diagnosis-20261107`.)

The diagnosis itself is made by controlled probes, not by file
inspection alone: a bare bot on the same libraries walking the
identical goal in seconds while a bare bot *as the citizen* moves
0.00 blocks proves the defect follows the citizen's server-side saved
state, not any client, driver, plugin, or terrain. (Ledger:
`character-body-diagnosis-20261107`.) Do not frame the defect as
server memory or a ghost state: a brand-new server process re-served
the identical position and vitals because it read them from the pool
on disk at join. (Ledger: `store-hunt-verdict-20261107`.)

## 3. The cure: pool-aside reset of a citizen's playerdata

The proven procedure for giving a citizen a genuinely fresh body.
Declare each step in the ledger before executing it. (Ledger:
`the-cure-declaration-20261107`.)

1. Confirm the citizen is offline. Take a full sha256 manifest of
   `world/players/data/` before touching anything.
2. Move the **entire** `world/players/data/` directory aside (all
   files, custody copies included). With no samples in the pool,
   `placeNewPlayer` falls through to a true fresh spawn.
   (Ledger: `the-cure-pool-move-20261107`.)
3. Discriminator: one bare join as the citizen. Expected result,
   measured: placement beside world spawn — (31.5, 70, −99.5) against
   spawn (32, 70, −96) — health 20, food 20, `onGround` true at every
   sample. Anything else means the mechanism is not what this file
   says; stop and re-investigate. (Ledger:
   `the-cure-discriminator-verdict-20261107`.)
4. **Save quirk:** while `world/players/data/` does not exist *as a
   directory*, Paper logs `Failed to save player data` at disconnect
   and writes nothing — measured twice, for a bare session and for a
   full character-loop session. Do not expect a fresh `.dat` from a
   join made while the directory is absent. (Ledger:
   `the-cure-save-behavior-20261107`.)
5. End-state, in this order: restore the pool directory first; delete
   **only** the citizen's loadable files (`.dat` and `.dat_old`);
   keep the named custody copies (`.bak-*`, `.frozen-original`,
   `.writethrough*`) in place as inert evidence. The citizen's next
   join then writes a genuinely fresh file into an existing
   directory. (Ledger: `the-cure-verdict-20261107`.)
6. Verify the new file by read-only NBT parse (Pos grounded, Health
   20, food 20, OnGround 1) and record its sha256.

Cure record for Cody, 2026-11-08: 91 files moved aside under manifest;
discriminator passed as predicted; end-state 90 files (89 pool + his
fresh `.dat`, sha256 `c8632cc2…`); deaths 38 at start, 38 at end, zero
operator-caused. (Ledger: `the-cure-verdict-20261107`.)

## 4. Server restarts and the filming surface

A server restart does **not** cure a playerdata fault on its own — the
sample pool on disk re-serves the state to the new process at the next
join (measured: byte-identical position and vitals from a brand-new
process). Restart is a step inside a cure, never the cure. (Ledger:
`server-restart-cure-20261107`, `server-restart-cure-verdict-20261107`.)

A restart also silently darkens the filming surface: **the headed
client (MagnusandMouse, Prism on the Orin desktop) does not rejoin by
itself.** It sits on a "Connection Lost / Server closed" dialog while
the cameraman process keeps issuing shot commands into a client that
is not in the world. Any brief that restarts the server must own the
client rejoin end-to-end, in the same sequence. (Record:
`~/cameraman/REJOIN-RESTORATION-2026-11-08.md` on the Orin; ledger:
`server-restart-cure-verdict-20261107`.)

Rejoin procedure, as proven 2026-11-08:

1. **STOP the cameraman first** (its own STOP mechanism) before any
   GUI work. Its keystrokes will otherwise land in whatever has
   focus — measured: a CUT burst opened Direct Connection and typed
   a command fragment into the Server Address field. This is the
   standing shared-keyboard hazard; it applies to *any* GUI work on
   the headed client, not only rejoins.
2. From the disconnect dialog: Back to Server List. The saved-server
   list may be empty; use **Direct Connection** and type `localhost`
   (the world is the Orin's own Paper server, port 25565).
   Screenshot-verify the field reads exactly `localhost` before
   joining.
3. Verify the rejoin in the **new** server process's log (a
   MagnusandMouse login line), then relaunch the cameraman by its
   proven pattern and verify its commands land as server-log entries,
   not merely as "sent" lines in its own log.

## 5. Serving the actor model (Qwen3 on :8311)

Pattern for a small resident actor model behind the league governor.
(Ledger: `casting-actor-server-20261107`,
`casting-governor-cody-actor-20261107`.)

- Convert: llama.cpp `convert_hf_to_gguf.py --outtype bf16`
  (Qwen3-0.6B BF16 → `~/models/Qwen3-0.6B-GGUF/qwen3-0.6b-bf16.gguf`).
- Serve under thor-admit with a declared memory peak
  (`serve qwen3-0.6b-actor --port 8311 --peak 4G`); llama-server flags:
  `-ngl 99 --ctx-size 8192 --parallel 4 --cont-batching`. Kill path:
  stop the thor-admit serve; nothing else references the port.
- Measured on this box: prompt processing ~1,300 tok/s, decode
  122–128 tok/s on actor-shaped calls.
- **Required client setting: `chat_template_kwargs:
  {enable_thinking: false}`.** Qwen3's default thinking mode spends
  the entire token budget in `reasoning_content` and returns empty
  content with `finish_reason: length`. With thinking disabled the
  same prompt returns a clean completion with `finish_reason: stop`.
  Any Qwen3 chat client in the league must send this.
- Governor identity: add the agent to `config/governor.json` with its
  own budget and an `agentUpstreams` override to the local server
  (pattern: `cody-actor` = 240 calls / 600 mind-seconds per rolling
  hour → `http://127.0.0.1:8311/v1`). Back up the config first; a
  governor restart clears its in-memory budget windows. Verify the
  override live (model list as the league identity) and confirm the
  first metered receipt in `ledger/mind-calls.jsonl` before calling
  the identity done. Existing identities' budgets and upstreams must
  be verified unchanged in the same write.

## 6. Waypoint arrival-radius deadlock

A waypoint that sits **inside** the gotoNear arrival radius will be
re-picked forever: gotoNear returns instant-ok (its start-distance
check passes at `range + 0.6` = 2.2 blocks for range 1.6), the
selection loop sees the nearest progress waypoint, selects it again,
and the body freezes bit-identical with zero failure lines. Dense
waypoint chains (~3-cell hops) create this by construction — adjacent
arrival radii overlap — where sparse chains masked it. (Ledger:
`shoulder-wedge-hopskip-20261107`.)

Fix, as landed in `character-plugin.js` (selection loop): a waypoint
within 2.2 blocks of the body, and not the anchor itself, counts as
consumed and is skipped. Rule for future route work: whenever a route
uses dense hops, audit hop spacing against the arrival radius, and
prefer surveyed per-cell feet-y hop chains over a single long walkTo
at the destination. (Ledger: `shoulder-wedge-fix-20261107`,
`shoulder-wedge-hopskip-20261107`.)

## 7. Trial safety: the death circuit-breaker

Standing floor rule for all Pulse trials: **a trial aborts
automatically at 3 deaths in the run** and is judged on the world
evidence gathered up to the abort. The mechanism is in the gate, not
in an operator watching: `trial.js` accepts `--max-deaths N`
(default 0 = off — always pass it explicitly), counts
`pulse/deaths.jsonl` records since the trial's baseline on every
15 s poll, and stamps the trial entry `abortedByCircuitBreaker` at
the threshold. Origin: Trial 7's corridor grind — 7 deaths in
3.5 minutes, the last four buying no information. (Ledger:
`death-circuit-breaker-20261107`.)

## 8. Daylight hold arithmetic

The cameraman holds the world at morning by re-issuing
`/time set 1000` on a refresh interval (`DAY_REFRESH_S` in
`~/cameraman/cameraman.py` on the Orin). The arithmetic that sizes
it: from time 1000, the world reaches 12000 (dusk) in ~550 s of wall
time. A ~600 s refresh therefore leaks night **every** cycle, and a
slipped cycle (one measured at 714 s) leaks minutes of mob-spawning
dark — the largest single killer in the world while it ran
(citizen deaths attributed in the tightening record: Geist 31 → 50,
Vega 7 → 20). The current setting is **300 s**, which peaks near
time-of-day ~7000, mid-morning, never near dusk. Rule: the refresh
interval must sit comfortably under the ~550 s leak window, with
margin for a slipped cycle. Real nights stay available on demand:
stop the cameraman and the hold lapses — night-exam choreography is
unaffected. (Record: `~/cameraman/DAYLIGHT-TIGHTENING-2026-11-07.md`
on the Orin, and the `DAY_REFRESH_S` note in `cameraman.py`. No
`pulse/ledger.jsonl` entry covers the tightening; the record and the
source are the citations.)

