## Watcher's Charter — first-pass instance (2026-11-08)

Night Study 4 (`~/workspace/studies/watchers-charter-20261107.md`)
distilled the hold-loss night into eight rules. This section is the
charter's first league instance: rules 2, 3 and 8 made real for the
current watcher set, with the compliance table filled from inspection
on 2026-11-08, worst rows stated as gaps rather than smoothed. The
cameraman's rule 1 (landing verification + HOLD-LOST alarm +
self-park, guarded build, incident record
`~/cameraman/INCIDENT-HOLD-LOSS-2026-11-08.md` on the Orin) predates
this pass and is the reference instance; its code and process were
**not** touched here — audited read-only only.

### What was placed

| Artefact | Where | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Heartbeat emitter | Thor `~/craftmind-league/meta-watcher/heartbeat_emitter.py`; Orin copy `~/meta-watcher/heartbeat_emitter.py` | Wrapper-side, stdlib-only. Reads each watcher's **output artefacts** (effects), never a bare pid — a live pid with a frozen artefact reads as dead, which is the curator-crash lesson |
| Meta-watcher | Thor `~/craftmind-league/meta-watcher/meta_watcher.py` | The league's simplest code: stdlib only, no shared libraries with the watchers. Reads heartbeat effect ages vs declared period + 2× grace; writes `commons/alarms/alarm-<watcher>-<epoch>.json` + a line in `commons/alarms/alarm.log`; writes/removes `commons/alarms/ALL-CLEAR.json` |
| Runner | `~/craftmind-league/meta-watcher/run-watchers.sh` | One invocation = emitter pass + staleness sweep |
| Heartbeat store | Thor `~/craftmind-league/commons/heartbeats/<watcher>.json` | A store no watcher owns. Orin beats (cameraman, capture) are **written on the Orin and shipped to the Thor** over the `thor-lan` ssh alias — rule 3, cross-host |
| Alarm store | Thor `~/craftmind-league/commons/alarms/` | Artefacts + `alarm.log` + `ALL-CLEAR.json` |
| Schedule | Thor systemd user timer `league-meta-watcher.timer` (5 min); Orin systemd user timer `league-heartbeat-orin.timer` (5 min) | The one declared background checker pair. Ledger: `watchers-charter-first-pass-20261108` |

Each heartbeat carries content, per rule 2: watcher name, host,
declared period, the **last verified effect in words**, and that
effect's timestamp. Staleness is judged on the *effect's* age, so a
heartbeat file that stops being refreshed — or a watcher whose
artefacts freeze while its process lives — both age into an alarm.

### Compliance table — tonight's truth (inspected 2026-11-08)

| Process | Rule 1 effect store | Rule 2 heartbeat → | Rule 4 black-box truth | Rule 6 safe state |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Cameraman | Minecraft server log landing lines (its own guard verifies each daylight send within 8 s) | Thor commons heartbeats, shipped from the Orin every 5 min; effect read = last `VERIFIED LANDED` line | World time inside the held band, read server-side (its guard); meta-watcher alarms if landings stop ageing-in | STOP self-park after 2 unverified sends + 1 failed recovery — **compliant, the reference row** |
| Curator | Manifest/reel artefacts on Thor disk (`~/curator/manifests/`) | Thor commons heartbeats; effect read = newest manifest entry | Newest manifest moment age vs its 600 s pulse — now meta-watched: **tonight's silent 06:37Z crash class (frozen artefacts, live-looking silence) now raises an alarm artefact + log line within ~35 min worst case** (600 s period + 2× grace + 5 min sweep). Black-box probe beyond the artefact age (reel content spot-check) still absent — partial | Pulse crash leaves offsets frozen + (since v1.1) no self-incident note; the alarm now comes from outside. Safe state = stops pulsing by dying, which is honest only because the meta-watcher now notices |
| Digest | `digest/digest.jsonl` growth + the citizens' state it digests | Thor commons heartbeats; effect read = last digest entry (tick + time-of-day) | A probe can read the newest digest entry's ts and compare to the server-log tail — the emitter *is* that read, every 5 min | **Gap:** no declared safe state; a dead digest fabricates nothing (it is read-only) but nothing in the digest itself announces the halt — detection is the meta-watcher's, mitigation is a citizen lane noticing stale world state |
| Governor | `ledger/mind-calls.jsonl` receipts vs the model servers' own logs | Thor commons heartbeats; effect read = newest receipt (agent + status) | **Partial / declared weakness:** the governor is demand-driven — receipt staleness cannot distinguish "governor down" from "nobody called". Declared period 900 s; the heartbeat states the ambiguity in its own detail field. A synthetic probe call (rule 4 proper) is **not** built — gap | Deny-by-budget behaviour is in its config; on unverified state it has no self-park. Gap, stated |
| GLM consult driver | Answer entries in `commons/consults/glm.jsonl` (+ consumption by the citizen loop, outside this beat) | Thor commons heartbeats; effect read = newest answer | Declared period 3600 s (answers are demand-driven; an answer within the active hour is the end-to-end proof). **Sharper gap:** a filed-but-unanswered consult aging past the driver's ~60 s poll has no dedicated alarm — the book must be read to see it. Consumption-side staleness (answers filed, never consumed) likewise unprobed — gap | None declared; a dead driver simply stops answering, now visible via this beat within period+grace |
| MiniMax consult driver | Answer entries in `commons/consults/minimax.jsonl` | Thor commons heartbeats; effect read = newest answer (first answer landed 2026-11-08T07:38Z, Vega's) | Same shape and same gaps as the GLM row | Same as GLM row |
| Capture (Orin) | Segment files growing on the Orin, `~/capture/segments.jsonl` | **Cross-host:** beat written on the Orin, shipped to the Thor commons every 5 min; effect read = newest segment start | Newest segment age < 2× its ~120 s cadence is checkable from the Thor-side beat — satisfied. A Thor-side read of the segment files themselves (content, not just the index) is not done — partial | systemd unit (`Restart=on-failure`) stops + the beat goes stale on the Thor if it dies; never overwrites old segments. Reasonably compliant |

**Worst rows, in order:** (1) Governor — no black-box probe exists; its
heartbeat can only report receipt flow, and its staleness is ambiguous
by construction. (2) Consult drivers — no filed-but-unanswered alarm;
the books are the evidence and nobody is paged by an aging question.
(3) Digest — no safe state of its own. (4) Meta-watcher rule 8c — its
own heartbeat (`commons/heartbeats/meta-watcher.json`) is written
every sweep, but **no external dead-man's-switch expecter is
registered**: if the Thor or the timer dies silently, the chain ends
in silence inside the fleet. That is the charter's own named
terminus and it is **not yet built** — stated here as the first
pass's largest single gap. Rule 8b is also partial: the meta-watcher
runs on the Thor only; the Orin runs no reciprocal sweep of
Thor-side heartbeats (its beats are consumed on the Thor). What is
cross-host tonight is the *evidence path* for Orin watchers, not the
checking itself.

### Meta-watcher runbook

- **Is it running?** `systemctl --user list-timers league-meta-watcher`
  on the Thor (5 min); `systemctl --user list-timers league-heartbeat-orin`
  on the Orin. One manual pass: `~/craftmind-league/meta-watcher/run-watchers.sh thor`.
- **Health at a glance:** `commons/alarms/ALL-CLEAR.json` present and
  fresh = every watcher fresh at last sweep. Absent + `alarm-*.json`
  artefacts = read the newest artefact and `alarm.log`.
- **An alarm names** the stale watcher, the reason (effect age vs
  period + grace), and its last verified effect — start diagnosis
  from that effect, not from the watcher's own log (that is the
  charter's whole point).
- **Known false-alarm shapes:** governor and the consult drivers are
  demand-driven; a quiet league (no calls, no consults) ages their
  beats by design. Their declared periods (900 s / 3600 s) are set
  wide for exactly this; if alarms fire on a genuinely idle night,
  the record should say "idle", not "down" — check receipts/books
  before restarting anything.
- **The meta-watcher itself:** its heartbeat is
  `commons/heartbeats/meta-watcher.json`. If that file's
  `emitted_at` is older than ~15 min, the checker pair has stopped —
  and per the gap above, nothing will announce it. A human glancing
  at that file is currently the last link in the chain.

### Rule-7 drill — first instance (ledger `watchers-charter-test-drill-20261108`)

Controlled, end-to-end, against a synthetic watcher only: a
`test-drill` heartbeat with `force_stale: true` was placed in
`commons/heartbeats/` at 2026-11-08T07:48:53Z. The meta-watcher
exited 3, wrote `commons/alarms/alarm-test-drill-1794124133.json`,
appended the alarm line to `alarm.log`, and removed `ALL-CLEAR.json`.
The test heartbeat was then deleted; the next sweep (07:49:01Z)
exited 0 and restored `ALL-CLEAR.json` naming all seven watchers
(cameraman, capture, curator, digest, glm-driver, governor,
minimax-driver). Drill artefacts are retained in `commons/alarms/`
as the record. Two implementation bugs were caught *by this
machinery running on real data* during the pass — ISO `+00:00`
timestamps the first staleness reader could not parse, and a
naive-vs-aware datetime clash on the cameraman's naive-UTC log
timestamps — both fixed, both re-verified live; they are recorded
here because a checker that has never failed on real input has not
been tested either.

