## Operator-skill launches — supervision is proven, not assumed (2026-11-08, Vega food-first lane, triple-011)

Three substrate rules, paid for with a citizen's food margin (food 3 -> 2, no
deaths, no damage — the margin bought the lesson, the citizen kept her record):

1. **Smoke-test the executor's own predicates before launch.** A lineage-b
   operator skill is launch-ready only when a Thor-side require passes the
   same checks `runSkill` applies: `typeof (mod.default || mod) ===
   "function"` and `typeof mod.post === "function"`. `node --check` proves
   syntax, not contract. Edit a working skill in place; never rewrite it
   from memory between runs — transcription re-introduces contract drift
   (export shape, require paths) and the fall-through charges the citizen.
2. **A watchdog is armed only when its armed-line is read back, naming the
   /proc-verified citizen pid.** A pidfile written by a compound launch line
   (`a && b & echo $!`) holds a shell-wrapper pid, not node. Verify
   `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` is the citizen process. On any latch failure the
   citizen falls through to the author loop within seconds and roams at her
   own risk until the floor parks her — a missing armed-line means the
   launch is unsupervised, full stop.
3. **Re-run pre-flight margin math whenever the margin changes.** Food (and
   hp) margins are spent by substrate failures too, not only by the plan.
   When expected burn meets the remaining margin, the deliverable is the
   written no-go — floors ratchet one way, and a plan that needs the good
   case at hp 3 is not a plan.

