## 9. The pristine-body verdict — the wedge is CELL-BOUND, not body-bound (2026-11-08, triple-016)

**The experiment (ACTIONS item 20; pre-registered `pristine-body-preregistered-20261108`).** A brand-new identity, PristineProbe, was made genuine by the §3 cure mechanics applied to the new name (116-file manifest → pool aside → discriminator join grounded at (42.5, 67, −104.5), hp/food 20/20, save failed on disconnect per the quirk → pool restored, manifest-verified) and given ONE walking session. It had never been cloned and never relocated — every body-side variable was zero.

**Result: it pinned, bit-identical, in its first session.** After five jittery opening legs through the shelf cluster (dispH 3.1/1.0/5.0/5.1/6.1 under 366–490 forcedMoves each), the body settled at **(41.699, 67, −84.318), cell (41,−85)** and never moved again: 14 route/pad legs dispH 0.005–0.151, 9 traverse legs 0–0.03, all 10 stance diagnostics 0–0.028 — including a raw flat-face push at **dispH 0.0 with 76 forcedMoves, numerically identical to Cody's genuine-body baseline** — and 8 park legs 0–0.022. Total **21,791 forcedMoves**, hp/food 20/20 throughout, rests quiet, **zero deaths, zero hostiles within 48 blocks at any rest** (same-hour census: all 23 saved hostiles subterranean; the five 09:24Z surface remnants absent from the saved store). Daylight hold landing-verified throughout the run (landings 21:14:25, 21:19:32, 21:24:43Z inside the window).

**Standing consequences.**
1. **Clone-inheritance and relocation history are exonerated as causes.** The clone-freeze signature occurs with NO cloning and NO relocation. Do not spend another lane on body provenance as the wedge explanation.
2. **The pin cell is shared across bodies:** (41,−85) wedged Geist's genuine attempt (41.161, 67, −84.927) and PristineProbe (41.699, 67, −84.318) hours apart; the stance pair (72–73, −101/−102) is Cody's instance. The wedge is a property of **cells on this Paper build**, not of citizens. Cody's genuine body crossed the adjacent shelf on the S1 morning (05:40Z) — so the discriminating variable is now **what differs at the wedge cells between that traversal and every later attempt** (cell-level world state vs a server-side change after the S1 window). That question is the next experiment's to design; it is NOT answered here, and the stance pair itself was never reached by the pristine body, so no stance-specific verdict exists.
3. **The staircase stays frozen** (its premise was stance-local; the wedge is wider than the stance pair). The dig-step lever stays OFF and untouched — both remain their owners' calls.
4. **PristineProbe is removed.** Its .dat was recorded (sha256 8f014fdb833729b2307ec19e9025b7e6f3acaa6c719a47bcb39c1ec311cca171) and deleted with its .dat_old per the pre-registered pin end-state; the pool is back to the 116-file manifest, byte-identical except the headed client's own live saves. Citizens untouched and sha-verified before/after: Cody e875776063946437d17a7348b79c1f8bf0c37d7938f31466de1a180d28ba5ca4, Geist 8ecbd23060cea01a83a944e6c75a2d283a89716edfe01e91b6181d4d760d94f0, Vega 4b65ff2e38a43de4da729610c5549151d15adc41d90ddf3db01f42cb52a8edc6.
5. **Tooling note:** mineflayer's `localPort` option does NOT pin the source port (createBot maps it to `localAddress`; both pristine joins arrived on unpinned ephemeral ports). The stance-probe-era "pinned port" records were never pinned. Source-port pinning requires `localAddress` + an explicit net-level bind and is not available through the current bot route — treat port-pinning as an open tooling gap, not a variable anyone has actually controlled.

### Wedge-cell discriminator addendum (2026-11-08, follow-on to triple-016; triple-017)

FORENSIC: Cody's 05:40Z S1 traversal NEVER crossed the pin cell. His relocate ran route-2 along the z=-101 row (survey corridor z-106..-88; the pin cell (41,-85) is outside it) using the RELOCATE driver (dense ~3-cell BFS hops); every failing attempt since used prims.gotoNear. The 05:40-vs-later contrast dissolves - different route AND different driver. Server timeline: ONE restart all day (04:54:06-42Z, before the crossing); all config files written at that boot; the only gamerule write all day was randomTickSpeed 3->9 at 19:53:24Z (after the Geist/stance failures, before PristineProbe).

SCRATCH TESTS (offline only; live world never touched; dig-step OFF; citizens' .dat shas verified identical before/after): identical walk driver over world copies on the Thor. F = live-world snapshot + the Orin's own Paperclip bundle (paper.jar, boots as Paper 26.1.2-74 @e4e17fc = the live binary family) + fleet JDK25: REPRODUCED THE LIVE PATHOLOGY IN FULL - veto storm on nearly every leg (fm 162-471), pin locus in the same spawn-shoulder cluster (settled ~(35.3,-100.7); approach to (41,-85) never completed), raw flat-face push dispH 0.0/fm 80 and raw +1 N 0.057 (live genuine baselines: 0.0/76, slide-south fail). G = same binary + FRESHLY GENERATED world + grass pad at the pin coordinates: NO pathology (crossings displaced metres; one 83-block relocation-style jump). D = same jar+world without a hostile sweep diverged independently (below) and is excluded from the contrast.

VERDICT (triple-017, confirmed): the veto is a property of the running 26.1.2 server interacting with THIS WORLD's saved state - not the pin cells' block geometry ((41,66,-85) is plain grass, air above), not the driver, not body lineage. Version skew is on the table: the world's chunks are saved at DataVersion 5023 (26.3) while the server that runs them boots 26.1.2-74; chunk (2,-6) carries InhabitedTime 2,087,388. Pins are cluster-shaped ((36,-83), (35.3,-100.7), (41,-85), (28,-100)), not single-cell.

MECHANISM M-3 (independent finding, proven on scratch D): a client logging into an area dense with saved entities can be halted at login state by the entity packet stream - the server logged the spawn, the client never fired its spawn event, position frozen at the previous session's disconnect point. The live world retains ~13,365 item entities server-side (citizens' death drops). Rejoining a citizen into an entity-dense area is a live hazard; sweep entities around a rejoin point first.

SETUP FAILURES FILED (this lane's own): first staging copied paper-26.3-242.jar (the staged upgrade file, mtime Nov 5 22:22) instead of the live Paperclip bundle paper.jar - the live banner 26.1.2-74 comes from paper.jar fetching cache/mojang_26.1.2.jar. System OpenJDK 25 SIGSEGV'd (vframeStreamForte, async-profiler frame) on the 26.3-migrated world in scratch D's first boot; the fleet JDK25 (~/lm-wt/agent-world/runtime/jdk-25) ran it clean. FIFO-fed consoles lose commands at server stop; RCON is the reliable control channel for scratch servers. NOTE for the record: OPERATIONS/triple epoch lines saying "Paper 26.1.2 build 74" describe the BOOT BANNER; chunk data in both the live world and world-26.3-backup is written by 26.3 - save-version provenance is messier than the banner.

OPEN VARIABLE (next experiment, not this lane): byte-level diff of chunk (2,-6) + neighbours between the Nov-5 backup and today's world; and a restart A/B on a CLONED copy of the live world under F's exact setup (fresh boot, re-run A2a) to separate runtime accumulation from saved-chunk-data as the veto's carrier. The live-restart A/B on the REAL server remains Casey's call.
